    "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    This paper investigates radio-frequency (RF) sheath rectification in strongly magnetized discharges under inclined magnetic field configurations using 1D3V particle-in-cell (PIC) simulations and an analytical plasma series resonance (PSR) model. We show that tilting the magnetic field toward grazing incidence (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mi>θ</mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>0</mml:mn>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    ) lowers the resonant frequency of the plasma-sheath system by effectively increasing the electron inertia along field lines. The dynamic response of the system is universally governed by the dimensionless parameter\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mi>χ</mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo>=</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mfrac>\n                            <mml:mrow>\n                              <mml:msub>\n                                <mml:mi>ω</mml:mi>\n                                <mml:mrow>\n                                  <mml:mi>pe</mml:mi>\n                                </mml:mrow>\n                              </mml:msub>\n                              <mml:mi>sin</mml:mi>\n                              <mml:mo>⁡</mml:mo>\n                              <mml:mi>θ</mml:mi>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>ω</mml:mi>\n                          </mml:mfrac>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    , which controls the interplay between the projected plasma frequency and the RF driving frequency\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mi>ω</mml:mi>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    . For large values (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mi>χ</mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo>≫</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>100</mml:mn>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    ), the normalized rectified potential\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:msub>\n                            <mml:mi>V</mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mrow>\n                              <mml:mtext>DC</mml:mtext>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                          </mml:msub>\n                          <mml:mo>−</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:msub>\n                            <mml:mi>V</mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mrow>\n                              <mml:mtext>fl</mml:mtext>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                          </mml:msub>\n                          <mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mo>/</mml:mo>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:msub>\n                            <mml:mi>V</mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mrow>\n                              <mml:mtext>RF</mml:mtext>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                          </mml:msub>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    follows the classical capacitive limit of\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mn>1</mml:mn>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mo>/</mml:mo>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mi>π</mml:mi>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    . As\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mi>χ</mml:mi>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    decreases, ion inertia effects cause the normalized potential to asymptote toward\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mn>1</mml:mn>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mo>/</mml:mo>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mn>2</mml:mn>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    . At intermediate values (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:msub>\n                            <mml:mi>χ</mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mrow>\n                              <mml:mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mml:mi>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                          </mml:msub>\n                          <mml:mo>≈</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>3</mml:mn>\n                          <mml:mtext>–</mml:mtext>\n                          <mml:mn>5</mml:mn>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    ), a strong resonant enhancement of\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:msub>\n                            <mml:mi>V</mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mrow>\n                              <mml:mtext>DC</mml:mtext>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                          </mml:msub>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    occurs, driven by the series resonance between the capacitive sheath and the inductive magnetized bulk. This potential peak is strongly attenuated as electron–neutral collisionality increases. Finally, for grazing angles near\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mi>χ</mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo>≃</mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>1</mml:mn>\n                        </mml:mrow>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    , the electron transit time through the sheath matches the RF period leading to a total collapse of the rectified potential. The analytical PSR model shows excellent agreement with PIC simulations across a wide of driving frequencies, RF voltage amplitudes, and discharge lengths. These results provide a robust predictive framework for controlling sheath potentials, impurity sourcing, and power coupling in industrial plasma sources and ion cyclotron resonance heating antennas in fusion devices.\n                  </jats:p>",
